00:00:11.963 --> 00:00:16.963
♪ There ain't no need ♪

00:00:17.952 --> 00:00:20.035
♪ Oh, ah ♪

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:34.060
- [Narrator] Aretha
Franklin, Beyonce, D'Angelo,

00:00:34.060 --> 00:00:35.903
John Legend, Anderson Paak.

00:00:35.903 --> 00:00:37.690
What do they all have in common?

00:00:37.690 --> 00:00:39.490
Like countless other black musicians,

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:41.810
they've all gained their
foundation and love for music

00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:45.371
through the black church and
the music it birthed, gospel.

00:00:45.371 --> 00:00:50.371
♪ Guide my feet Lord ♪

00:00:53.572 --> 00:00:55.600
♪ Prayers when I'm hungry ♪

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.250
♪ I've never eaten ♪

00:00:57.250 --> 00:00:59.130
It's Sunday and I'm going to church.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:01.090
I'm in Orlando, Florida at Live Church,

00:01:01.090 --> 00:01:03.130
where Grammy winning artist
and gospel music star

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:05.236
Tye Tribbett leads the congregation.

00:01:05.236 --> 00:01:07.819
(gospel music)

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:14.830
- Mainstream, secular, gospel,

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:17.430
everybody seems to come
through the church to like

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:19.240
hone their gifts, you
know 'cause that's a place

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:20.810
where a lot of people are accepted.

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:22.000
You can make mistakes and not,

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.000
cause you're not on TV reading charts.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.600
You're just like trying to figure it out.

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:29.357
♪ Has more power than you ♪

00:01:29.357 --> 00:01:30.190
♪ Nobody ♪

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:32.189
♪ Nobody ♪

00:01:32.189 --> 00:01:33.347
♪ Nobody ♪

00:01:33.347 --> 00:01:35.190
♪ Nobody ♪

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:36.896
So many musicians that I know

00:01:36.896 --> 00:01:39.870
literally came through GA and Soundcheck

00:01:39.870 --> 00:01:41.493
actually that we see now.

00:01:43.041 --> 00:01:43.907
♪ Nobody ♪

00:01:43.907 --> 00:01:44.740
♪ Said nobody ♪

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:45.573
♪ Nobody ♪

00:01:45.573 --> 00:01:46.556
♪ Nobody ♪

00:01:46.556 --> 00:01:48.620
♪ Nobody ♪ ♪ Raise your book and say ♪

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:51.690
Music in the church is unconventional

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:53.230
and like I said, it's not charted.

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:55.270
You can kind of like
try things and be like,

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:56.392
weird, but it's okay
- It worked, I liked it.

00:01:56.392 --> 00:01:58.262
- [Tye] Put the bass there, okay.

00:01:58.262 --> 00:02:01.070
In the church, you can make those mistakes

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:03.346
that turn out to be
genius in another field.

00:02:03.346 --> 00:02:05.537
(screaming)

00:02:05.537 --> 00:02:06.471
♪ Who's greater than God ♪

00:02:06.471 --> 00:02:07.304
♪ Nobody ♪

00:02:07.304 --> 00:02:08.320
♪ Who's greater than God ♪

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:09.245
♪ Nobody ♪

00:02:09.245 --> 00:02:10.366
♪ Who's greater than Jesus ♪

00:02:10.366 --> 00:02:12.032
♪ Nobody ♪ ♪ Who's greater than Jesus ♪

00:02:12.032 --> 00:02:12.865
♪ Nobody ♪

00:02:12.865 --> 00:02:14.956
♪Nobody greater ♪

00:02:14.956 --> 00:02:17.789
(airplane engine)

00:02:19.668 --> 00:02:20.780
- [Woman] LA and I are in Chicago

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:22.580
where the work of gospel pioneers like

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:24.540
Thomas Dorsey and Mahalia Jackson

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:27.150
gave rise to what we know as gospel music.

00:02:27.150 --> 00:02:30.000
But the city doesn't only
hold gospel music's past.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.510
Chicago gospel musicians
are constantly pushing

00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:35.610
this great art form and
their skills forward.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:37.730
- Definitely Chicago
is the home for gospel.

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:38.563
Hands down.

00:02:38.563 --> 00:02:39.396
- Okay, so we in the right spot.

00:02:39.396 --> 00:02:40.229
- [Donald] Yeah.

00:02:40.229 --> 00:02:41.950
- [Narrator] So talk
about the black church

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:43.970
being a training ground for musicians.

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:47.190
- It's a training ground
but also too, everyone is so

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:48.320
on top of their skill,

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:49.730
when not really knowing the raw skill

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:53.415
is so great that it's like
singing at the Apollo.

00:02:53.415 --> 00:02:55.396
(screaming)

00:02:55.396 --> 00:02:56.702
♪ What's his name ♪

00:02:56.702 --> 00:02:57.535
♪ Jesus ♪

00:02:57.535 --> 00:02:58.452
♪ What's his name ♪

00:02:58.452 --> 00:02:59.285
♪ Jesus ♪

00:02:59.285 --> 00:03:00.118
♪ What's his name ♪

00:03:00.118 --> 00:03:00.951
♪ Jesus ♪

00:03:00.951 --> 00:03:04.140
- You have to be good, like,
you don't have a choice.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:06.160
Or else, you just don't
get up there, you know.

00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:08.100
So that's why some of the great singers

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:09.590
like an Aretha or like Whitney

00:03:09.590 --> 00:03:11.170
or like so many came out of church

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:12.560
because we hear it all the time.

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:14.940
And it's others that you haven't heard

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:16.300
that probably equally as good

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:18.654
that you will never hear.
- [Narrator] True.

00:03:18.654 --> 00:03:19.662
♪ Jesus ♪

00:03:19.662 --> 00:03:21.280
♪ In the name of Jesus ♪

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:22.113
♪ Jesus ♪

00:03:22.113 --> 00:03:25.223
♪ In the name of Jesus ♪

00:03:25.223 --> 00:03:26.980
- I think the musicians

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:29.000
of this time, are so skilled.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:30.610
Like they're incredibly skilled.

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:33.190
Their ears are not the
same but their ability

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:34.330
is like out of here.

00:03:34.330 --> 00:03:36.870
It really is out of here, really is out.

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:38.823
I always tell 'em ya'll
make it too complicated.

00:03:38.823 --> 00:03:39.656
- Yeah yeah.

00:03:39.656 --> 00:03:41.562
- I always say you're overthinking,

00:03:41.562 --> 00:03:43.012
it's not that complicated,

00:03:43.012 --> 00:03:44.138
(laughing)

00:03:44.138 --> 00:03:44.971
it's really just, you know,

00:03:44.971 --> 00:03:46.308
two, three, six, five,

00:03:46.308 --> 00:03:47.590
then back to one.

00:03:47.590 --> 00:03:48.713
But you're trying to make it

00:03:48.713 --> 00:03:50.747
like a thirteenth
- Like explosions (laughs)

00:03:50.747 --> 00:03:51.580
- and then trying to expand it too much.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:53.429
Like it's not that complicated.

00:03:53.429 --> 00:03:57.790
(piano music)
(humming)

00:03:57.790 --> 00:03:58.920
Right there let me show you what

00:03:58.920 --> 00:04:01.460
they would do in church now.

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:02.978
The melody is,

00:04:02.978 --> 00:04:04.730
♪ All that is within me ♪

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:06.380
Instead of going to (piano music)

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:07.810
Yeah instead of going to that 6th.

00:04:07.810 --> 00:04:08.847
Right.

00:04:08.847 --> 00:04:09.680
- Yeah.

00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:11.748
(humming)

00:04:11.748 --> 00:04:12.839
Exactly.

00:04:12.839 --> 00:04:14.172
Then you go to the next chord,

00:04:14.172 --> 00:04:15.107
the chord is there.

00:04:15.107 --> 00:04:16.331
- That's the sweet spot.

00:04:16.331 --> 00:04:18.200
♪ Is is ♪

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:20.050
- So who created black
gospel music? (piano music)

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:21.330
Many historians will tell you

00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:23.490
it was Thomas A. Dorsey.

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:26.760
He's now regarded as the
father of black gospel music.

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:29.460
But Dorsey's career didn't
start out in the church.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:32.230
Born in Georgia, Dorsey
began as a blues musician

00:04:32.230 --> 00:04:34.500
and went by the name Georgia Tom.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:36.050
He toured alongside blues legend

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:37.680
Ma Rainey, and wrote hundreds of

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:39.400
blues and jazz songs.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:40.610
Some were a little risque,

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:43.247
like the hit he wrote
with guitarist Tampa Red,

00:04:43.247 --> 00:04:45.684
"It's Tight Like That" from 1928.

00:04:45.684 --> 00:04:47.108
♪ It's tight like that ♪

00:04:47.108 --> 00:04:47.941
♪ Beedle-um-Bum ♪

00:04:47.941 --> 00:04:49.190
♪ It's tight like that ♪

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:51.510
- In the 1920s Dorsey had an epiphany.

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:52.510
He began to turn away from

00:04:52.510 --> 00:04:55.130
secular music and started
writing gospel songs.

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:56.680
But he didn't leave his past behind.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:58.580
He often infused his gospel music

00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:01.100
with the feel and structure of the blues.

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:02.820
His gospel song, "If You See My Savior"

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:04.060
is swung and upbeat.

00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:06.380
It follows a 16-bar
blues chord progression.

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:08.190
It has a steady 4/4 time signature

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:09.740
that you can easily stomp your feet to.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:12.290
His lyrics, however, are
more hopeful and uplifting

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:13.640
than most blues songs.

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:15.380
Dorsey is offering a touching send-off

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:16.640
to someone who is about to depart

00:05:16.640 --> 00:05:17.720
to the other side.

00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:19.503
♪ Oh, tell them I am ♪

00:05:19.503 --> 00:05:22.520
♪ Coming home someday ♪

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:25.390
- In 1932, Dorsey came here
to Pilgrim Baptist Church

00:05:25.390 --> 00:05:27.250
in Chicago where he served as

00:05:27.250 --> 00:05:29.610
Music Director for more than 40 years.

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:33.177
In 2006, this historic church
building was gutted by fire,

00:05:33.177 --> 00:05:35.971
and the whole congregation
moved across the street.

00:05:35.971 --> 00:05:36.841
(organ music)

00:05:36.841 --> 00:05:37.897
♪ Through the storm ♪

00:05:37.897 --> 00:05:39.124
♪ Through the rain ♪

00:05:39.124 --> 00:05:41.861
♪ People walkin' in with Jesus' name ♪

00:05:41.861 --> 00:05:44.388
♪ Though darkness overflowing ♪

00:05:44.388 --> 00:05:47.221
♪ My Lord is nigh ♪

00:05:48.670 --> 00:05:50.380
- That's one of the things

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:52.370
that made Professor Dorsey famous,

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:57.370
was that he combined the
blues and church music

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:02.530
and gave it that rhythm and swing

00:06:02.530 --> 00:06:06.050
that a lot of people weren't ready for.

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:07.990
- Dorsey also employs call and response

00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:09.230
in many of his songs.

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:10.662
An element in African music where

00:06:10.662 --> 00:06:12.338
one phrase is the call,

00:06:12.338 --> 00:06:15.030
and a different phrase is the answer.

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:16.897
Like in his song, "Never Turn Back."

00:06:16.897 --> 00:06:18.373
♪ A long ♪

00:06:18.373 --> 00:06:19.765
♪ Yes Lord ♪

00:06:19.765 --> 00:06:20.761
♪ Time ago ♪

00:06:20.761 --> 00:06:22.310
♪ Yes Lord ♪

00:06:22.310 --> 00:06:27.000
- Call and response is
historical from slavery times

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:30.410
because blacks could
not read at that time.

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:35.350
And there was always one
person around who could.

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:37.063
And they would read it,

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:41.130
and everyone else would
repeat it behind them.

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:44.340
- In the morning, when I rise.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:46.620
Or I'm gonna lay down my burdens.

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:48.787
- Down by the riverside.
- You know what I'm sayin'?

00:06:48.787 --> 00:06:51.520
Call and response is
integral to gospel music.

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:53.270
It can be found in secular music as well.

00:06:53.270 --> 00:06:56.108
From artists like James
Brown to Kendrick Lamar.

00:06:56.108 --> 00:06:56.941
♪ Sit down ♪

00:06:56.941 --> 00:06:57.774
♪ Drank ♪

00:06:57.774 --> 00:06:58.607
♪ Stand up ♪

00:06:58.607 --> 00:06:59.440
♪ Drank ♪

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:00.273
♪ Pass out ♪

00:07:00.273 --> 00:07:01.106
♪ Drank ♪

00:07:01.106 --> 00:07:02.050
- Also in 1932,

00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:04.910
Dorsey lost his wife and
son during childbirth.

00:07:04.910 --> 00:07:06.920
This tragedy led him to
write what is considered

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:09.417
one of the greatest
gospel songs ever written,

00:07:09.417 --> 00:07:11.433
"Take My Hand Precious Lord"

00:07:11.433 --> 00:07:14.100
♪ Precious Lord ♪

00:07:19.688 --> 00:07:20.867
Unlike "If You Meet My Savior",

00:07:20.867 --> 00:07:22.770
"Precious Lord" maintains the emotion

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:25.440
of the blues without
adhering to its structure.

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:27.540
Check out Dorsey himself
performing the song

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:31.127
in the 1998 documentary,
"The Story of Gospel Music."

00:07:32.639 --> 00:07:35.556
♪ When I look back ♪

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:41.650
- Just to see him, we stood in awe of him.

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:43.130
You know, that was history.

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:45.500
- [Woman] However, Dorsey
was not always celebrated.

00:07:45.500 --> 00:07:46.950
- Professor Dorsey came,

00:07:46.950 --> 00:07:51.851
actually from Ebenezer Baptist
Church where he started.

00:07:51.851 --> 00:07:56.851
But they did not allow him to do his music

00:07:57.110 --> 00:07:58.960
the way he wanted to do it.

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:00.310
So he left there.

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:02.450
And came to Pilgrim.

00:08:02.450 --> 00:08:04.450
- He faced opposition for blending blues

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:06.060
and gospel with some ministers

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:08.190
calling it the devil's music.

00:08:08.190 --> 00:08:10.800
- A lot of the old time church folks

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:13.013
were saying that he was
playing the devil's music.

00:08:13.013 --> 00:08:15.130
That it wasn't of God.

00:08:15.130 --> 00:08:18.650
- Well I mean, you can
liken it until today.

00:08:18.650 --> 00:08:22.002
When we're doing hip hop.

00:08:22.002 --> 00:08:25.387
And so, you know, a lot of old people say,

00:08:25.387 --> 00:08:27.500
"That's the old devil's music."

00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:28.333
No.

00:08:28.333 --> 00:08:29.850
It's just a new style

00:08:30.726 --> 00:08:33.758
that's come to us to give the Lord praise.

00:08:33.758 --> 00:08:36.445
(organ music)

00:08:36.445 --> 00:08:38.695
(clapping)

00:08:39.590 --> 00:08:41.769
- Let's stand for prayer.

00:08:41.769 --> 00:08:43.849
(laughing)

00:08:43.849 --> 00:08:45.620
- God we thank you.

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:47.370
- Okay we're ready to finish, amen.

00:08:49.180 --> 00:08:51.070
- As far as the blending of genres,

00:08:51.070 --> 00:08:52.750
some people feel like it waters down

00:08:52.750 --> 00:08:53.970
the gospel message.

00:08:53.970 --> 00:08:56.420
- Message is words and sound is sound.

00:08:56.420 --> 00:08:58.010
So I don't understand how you can take

00:08:58.010 --> 00:08:59.770
a message down with sound.

00:08:59.770 --> 00:09:01.410
I think more so religion does that

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:03.740
versus spirituality or even God,

00:09:03.740 --> 00:09:04.960
because when God made music,

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:06.897
he didn't say, "This bass part is secular

00:09:06.897 --> 00:09:08.160
"and this bass part is gospel."

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:09.490
We did that.

00:09:09.490 --> 00:09:12.020
- Blending gospel and secular
sounds hasn't stopped.

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:14.690
Pioneers like Andrae
Crouch and Kirk Franklin

00:09:14.690 --> 00:09:16.460
have been mixing gospel with everything

00:09:16.460 --> 00:09:19.086
from funk to rock to hip hop.

00:09:19.086 --> 00:09:20.987
♪ My brother can't you see ♪

00:09:20.987 --> 00:09:23.189
♪ I've got the victory ♪

00:09:23.189 --> 00:09:24.022
♪ Stomp ♪

00:09:24.022 --> 00:09:25.280
It's this cross-pollination of genres

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:26.320
that makes gospel music

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:28.010
so adaptable to the times,

00:09:28.010 --> 00:09:30.510
and why gospel musicians are so versatile.

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:31.343
We play it all.

00:09:32.787 --> 00:09:34.038
♪ Everybody let's go ♪

00:09:34.038 --> 00:09:35.896
♪ Rejoice ♪

00:09:35.896 --> 00:09:38.650
♪ Rejoice to the Lord always ♪

00:09:38.650 --> 00:09:40.090
Can you speak on how

00:09:40.090 --> 00:09:43.620
your church has embraced
this new wave of music?

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:46.501
The shared exchange between gospel music

00:09:46.501 --> 00:09:48.637
and secular music.

00:09:48.637 --> 00:09:50.760
It's an exchange of influence.

00:09:50.760 --> 00:09:51.593
- Mm-hmm.

00:09:52.622 --> 00:09:54.343
♪ Lift your praise rejoice ♪

00:09:54.343 --> 00:09:56.060
- It's like how my music

00:09:56.060 --> 00:09:57.200
is outside of the church.

00:09:57.200 --> 00:09:59.060
I think it's a little bit for everybody.

00:09:59.060 --> 00:10:00.870
I think the biggest problem I had,

00:10:00.870 --> 00:10:01.980
even at Sony Records,

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:04.400
was what category do we put this guy in.

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:07.590
Is this hip hop gospel, is this...

00:10:07.590 --> 00:10:09.790
So we just coined this
phrase Kingdom Music.

00:10:14.646 --> 00:10:17.520
♪ Again I say rejoice ♪

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:18.710
We didn't want to alienate anybody.

00:10:18.710 --> 00:10:21.540
And we didn't want to
become elitist either.

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:22.620
It's very very broad.

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:23.940
You can get old-school tambourine,

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:25.170
chicken-eating feel.
- [Narrator] Church.

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:27.810
- Yeah, Hillsong Worship feel
when you just honor the Lord,

00:10:27.810 --> 00:10:29.280
you know what I'm saying?

00:10:29.280 --> 00:10:32.470
Or we can get straight street.

00:10:32.470 --> 00:10:34.710
We embrace all forms of music,

00:10:34.710 --> 00:10:36.110
so we can reach all forms of people

00:10:36.110 --> 00:10:37.070
with one message.

00:10:37.070 --> 00:10:37.903
- [Narrator] Indeed.

00:10:39.192 --> 00:10:42.420
- I came from the shed, jam session thing.

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:44.410
I just had like sheds, just jam sessions

00:10:44.410 --> 00:10:45.480
at the house all the time.

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:50.480
And from those pieces of grooves

00:10:51.320 --> 00:10:52.600
that we locked into,

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:55.430
I wrote songs, like no way
came from a shed groove.

00:10:55.430 --> 00:10:56.330
- [Narrator] Okay.

00:10:57.865 --> 00:10:59.614
(Tye beatboxing)

00:10:59.614 --> 00:11:01.454
(laughing)

00:11:01.454 --> 00:11:02.760
- Come on man.

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:04.070
- He's ready.

00:11:04.070 --> 00:11:04.930
- [Narrator] The term "woodshedding"

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:08.080
gained popularity among
jazz musicians in the 1930s.

00:11:08.080 --> 00:11:09.310
It means to go some place,

00:11:09.310 --> 00:11:10.630
like behind the wood shed,

00:11:10.630 --> 00:11:13.000
to practice your skills
where no one else can hear.

00:11:13.000 --> 00:11:13.833
(jazz music)

00:11:13.833 --> 00:11:15.110
But in the early 2000s,

00:11:15.110 --> 00:11:16.430
shedding became something new

00:11:16.430 --> 00:11:17.890
thanks to Gerald Forrest,

00:11:17.890 --> 00:11:20.610
the creator of a website
called Gospel Chops.

00:11:20.610 --> 00:11:22.790
Originally, the website
was going to focus on

00:11:22.790 --> 00:11:24.670
church organists and pianists,

00:11:24.670 --> 00:11:26.470
before it shifted its focus to drummers

00:11:26.470 --> 00:11:28.510
after he uploaded a video of Eric Moore

00:11:28.510 --> 00:11:30.050
breaking down, what was arguably,

00:11:30.050 --> 00:11:31.956
the first ever documented gospel chop.

00:11:31.956 --> 00:11:35.123
(Eric Moore drumming)

00:11:36.360 --> 00:11:39.870
In the mid-2000s, gospel
chops became a phenomenon.

00:11:39.870 --> 00:11:41.550
Tony and Thomas' shed is arguably

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:43.750
one of the most memorable
sheds of all time.

00:11:43.750 --> 00:11:45.710
Here, one can see those integral parts

00:11:45.710 --> 00:11:46.670
of gospel music.

00:11:46.670 --> 00:11:47.910
Tony and Thomas are responding

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:49.430
to each other's rhythmic calls

00:11:49.430 --> 00:11:51.030
and are doing so on the spot.

00:11:51.030 --> 00:11:53.447
(drum music)

00:11:56.340 --> 00:11:58.770
- So a question for
both of you, basically.

00:11:58.770 --> 00:12:00.560
I've never been to a shed session.

00:12:00.560 --> 00:12:02.040
I'm not really sure,

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:03.900
I've since learned about it,

00:12:03.900 --> 00:12:07.340
but I don't know what to expect.

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:08.962
What can I (laughs)

00:12:08.962 --> 00:12:10.953
- [Narrator] Oh, it'll be a
vibe though. It'll be a vibe.

00:12:10.953 --> 00:12:12.769
So it's like, I'll have the
quick tracks running in the app,

00:12:12.769 --> 00:12:14.839
and I'll blast it

00:12:14.839 --> 00:12:15.672
through the monitors

00:12:15.672 --> 00:12:16.505
- [Donald] Wow.

00:12:16.505 --> 00:12:17.338
- so everybody can hear
it, and keep up with it.

00:12:17.338 --> 00:12:18.171
- [Donald] That's cool.

00:12:18.171 --> 00:12:19.004
- [Narrator] Yeah.

00:12:19.004 --> 00:12:20.410
- [Woman Narrator] And that's happening

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:21.243
at seven tonight, eight?

00:12:21.243 --> 00:12:22.210
- [Narrator] We'll get there at 8.

00:12:22.210 --> 00:12:23.563
- [Woman Narrator] How
many people are coming?

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:25.473
- [Narrator] I'm hoping,
I'm trying to make it

00:12:25.473 --> 00:12:26.306
to the party.

00:12:26.306 --> 00:12:27.411
- [Woman Narrator] Oh.

00:12:27.411 --> 00:12:29.911
(drums music)

00:12:31.950 --> 00:12:33.820
- [Narrator] For gospel
drummers like myself,

00:12:33.820 --> 00:12:36.541
nothing sharpens skills
more than shedding.

00:12:36.541 --> 00:12:37.870
(narrator drumming)

00:12:37.870 --> 00:12:39.100
This is how shedding works.

00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:40.550
There are two simple rules.

00:12:40.550 --> 00:12:42.400
One, you establish a groove,

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:44.930
and two, trade solos
while keeping the one,

00:12:44.930 --> 00:12:46.170
or the downbeat.

00:12:46.170 --> 00:12:47.003
Locking in the groove

00:12:47.003 --> 00:12:48.170
is an important step in shedding

00:12:48.170 --> 00:12:49.800
because it gives context to all of the

00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:51.970
chops, licks, and feels.

00:12:51.970 --> 00:12:52.950
The groove is the template

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:54.230
that we all work within,

00:12:54.230 --> 00:12:56.626
and it allows the trading
of solos to make sense.

00:12:56.626 --> 00:12:59.307
(drum music)

00:12:59.307 --> 00:13:00.930
Depending on the tempo of the groove,

00:13:00.930 --> 00:13:03.230
drummers will solo for
either four or eight bars.

00:13:03.230 --> 00:13:05.060
Four bars for slower tempos,

00:13:05.060 --> 00:13:06.831
eight bars for faster tempos.

00:13:06.831 --> 00:13:09.498
(drums beating)

00:13:20.182 --> 00:13:22.883
(piano music)

00:13:22.883 --> 00:13:26.873
What started as musicians
jamming after church services,

00:13:26.873 --> 00:13:30.639
turned into a safe haven
for young black musicians.

00:13:30.639 --> 00:13:33.306
(drums beating)

00:13:34.440 --> 00:13:35.670
This adaptability continues

00:13:35.670 --> 00:13:38.037
to push gospel music into
new musical territory.

00:13:38.037 --> 00:13:40.704
(drums beating)

00:13:41.971 --> 00:13:42.990
(piano music)

00:13:42.990 --> 00:13:44.760
And in churches all across the country,

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:46.240
the next generation of musicians are

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:47.684
shedding their skills.

00:13:47.684 --> 00:13:50.351
(drums beating)

00:13:52.140 --> 00:13:54.050
Just look at Justin Wilson II,

00:13:54.050 --> 00:13:55.517
better know as the drumming prodigy,

00:13:55.517 --> 00:13:57.433
@babyboydrummer on Instagram.

00:13:58.519 --> 00:14:00.769
(drumming)

00:14:02.359 --> 00:14:04.820
(people cheering)

00:14:04.820 --> 00:14:07.380
Here, he plays alongside
notable gospel musicians

00:14:07.380 --> 00:14:09.680
Robert Bubby Lewis and Justin Raines

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:12.000
at the 2019 NAMM Convention.

00:14:12.000 --> 00:14:13.300
The young drummer holds his own

00:14:13.300 --> 00:14:14.780
alongside the other musicians

00:14:14.780 --> 00:14:17.168
in what is essentially a shed session.

00:14:17.168 --> 00:14:19.418
(drumming)

00:14:20.550 --> 00:14:21.900
The exchange is a testament

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:23.180
to the black gospel church as a

00:14:23.180 --> 00:14:25.365
training ground for artists and musicians.

00:14:25.365 --> 00:14:27.615
(drumming)

00:14:29.730 --> 00:14:31.700
What gospel music is and sounds like

00:14:31.700 --> 00:14:32.700
continues to change.

00:14:34.120 --> 00:14:34.953
But there's no denying,

00:14:34.953 --> 00:14:36.080
that the black gospel church provides

00:14:36.080 --> 00:14:37.920
a foundation for future musicians

00:14:37.920 --> 00:14:38.753
who will one day,

00:14:38.753 --> 00:14:40.218
share their gifts with the world.

00:14:40.218 --> 00:14:41.557
♪ Greater, greater, greater, greater ♪

00:14:41.557 --> 00:14:43.273
♪ Greater, greater, greater ♪

00:14:43.273 --> 00:14:45.155
♪ Greater, greater ♪

00:14:45.155 --> 00:14:47.140
♪ Greater than anything ♪

00:14:47.140 --> 00:14:48.310
- But the gospel music industry,

00:14:48.310 --> 00:14:51.480
I just, I mean, it's so open

00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:52.610
it can be anything now.

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:54.710
I like that it's not just choir robes now.

00:14:54.710 --> 00:14:57.530
It's so vast because everybody's not just

00:14:57.530 --> 00:14:58.570
exposed to their lane.

00:14:58.570 --> 00:15:00.917
Everybody's exposed to everything.

00:15:00.917 --> 00:15:04.334
♪ Greater than anything ♪

00:15:05.840 --> 00:15:09.640
- So there must be a culture
of working extremely hard

00:15:09.640 --> 00:15:13.620
to get to that level surrounding
this community, right?

00:15:13.620 --> 00:15:15.260
- I don't even know if
it's work to get there.

00:15:15.260 --> 00:15:17.170
I think that because that's all you hear,

00:15:17.170 --> 00:15:18.220
it just shows up.

00:15:18.220 --> 00:15:19.053
- It's the, yeah,

00:15:19.053 --> 00:15:20.640
you're around those church services

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:21.810
every day of the week.

00:15:21.810 --> 00:15:23.150
- Yeah, like if you hear somebody

00:15:23.150 --> 00:15:24.830
speaking another language
throughout your life,

00:15:24.830 --> 00:15:26.230
then you speak it.

00:15:26.230 --> 00:15:27.205
♪ You're greater than anything ♪

00:15:27.205 --> 00:15:29.956
♪ We look up to you ♪ ♪
Where our help comes from ♪

00:15:29.956 --> 00:15:31.159
♪ You're greater ♪

00:15:31.159 --> 00:15:33.152
♪ Greater than anything ♪

00:15:33.152 --> 00:15:35.025
- [Donald] It's just a
part of the community

00:15:35.025 --> 00:15:35.858
so you just do it, you know?

00:15:35.858 --> 00:15:37.938
You just learn how to dance
because everybody's dancing.

00:15:37.938 --> 00:15:38.771
- [Woman Narrator] It's the norm.

00:15:38.771 --> 00:15:39.604
- [Donald] It's the norm.

00:15:39.604 --> 00:15:41.452
It's very much a norm.

00:15:41.452 --> 00:15:43.510
♪ One more time say ♪

00:15:43.510 --> 00:15:45.114
♪ One more time say ♪

00:15:45.114 --> 00:15:46.702
♪ He's greater ♪

00:15:46.702 --> 00:15:50.294
♪ Greater than anything ♪

00:15:50.294 --> 00:15:52.998
- Hey, give God glory in here.

00:15:52.998 --> 00:15:56.331
(congregation clapping)

